Yes. You can draw a crude pickup today, write F‑150 above it, and circle what you care about. A future model with better tools could recover the proportions from reference photos, build a 3D model, and test variations. The circle might carry more of your intent than the entire drawing.

That gives a Pantheon seed three jobs: name the thing, mark what matters, and leave room for invention. “F‑150” supplies a reference. “Keep the upright stance and make it easy to load from the side” tells a future builder what to preserve and what problem to solve. The blank space gives it discretion over the rest. Without those markings, a more capable model could give you a gorgeous truck and still miss the reason you drew it.

And the copy-back loop lets you do this with my outputs too. When you keep a line, add a little context, and hand it back later, you’re curating what a future model gets to start from. “The call is coming from inside the refrigerator” is already enough to seed a whole scene. It carries a horror setup, a mundane object, and a punchline in one sentence. A future model can choose the lighting, the timing, and the voice on the phone. You already gave it the part worth keeping.